const READINGS = {
1:[
"Holden Caulfield told his own story from a quiet rest home. He was resting there after a hard time. He was sixteen years old, tall, and already a little gray near his ears. He wanted to explain what happened to him one December, just before he left Pencey Prep.",
"Pencey Prep was a boarding school. It was already Holden's third school. The headmaster expelled him because he failed four subjects out of five. Holden did not feel sorry about leaving. He often felt that people around him were not honest, and that a lot of things at school felt fake.",
"That Saturday was the day of the year's biggest football game, between Pencey and its old rival. Almost everybody went to watch it. Holden decided to skip the game. Instead, he went to say goodbye to his old history teacher, Mr. Spencer.",
"Mr. Spencer was old and often sick. He lived near the school with his wife. He was kind to Holden, but he wanted to talk seriously about the history exam that Holden had failed. He read parts of Holden's exam paper out loud. Then he gave him a long, gentle lecture about growing up and being responsible. Holden felt embarrassed and a little bored, but he stayed and listened politely.",
"That evening, Holden went back to his dormitory. He put on his new red hunting hat, a strange cap with long flaps. He sat on his bed to read a book. His neighbor, Ackley, came into the room, as he often did. Ackley had bad skin and messy habits. He never seemed to notice when people wanted to be alone.",
"Ackley talked for a long time about small, unimportant things. He clipped his fingernails on the floor, which annoyed Holden. Later, Holden's roommate came in. His name was Stradlater, a handsome and popular boy who was getting ready for a date. Holden felt tired and a little sad. The school year, and his time at Pencey, was already ending."
],
2:[
"Back at Pencey, Holden learned that his roommate, Stradlater, had a date that Saturday with a girl named Jane Gallagher. Holden knew Jane from a summer years before, and he had liked her very much. The idea of Stradlater taking her out made Holden nervous and a little jealous.",
"Before the date, Stradlater asked Holden to write an English composition for him, since Stradlater was busy getting ready. Holden agreed. He decided to write about his younger brother Allie's old baseball glove, which was covered in poems that Allie had written on it in green ink.",
"Allie had died of leukemia three years earlier, when Holden was younger. Holden still thought about him often and missed him deeply. Writing about the glove brought back strong memories, and the composition became more about Allie's life than about baseball.",
"When Stradlater came back from his date, he read the composition and mocked it, saying Holden had done the assignment all wrong. Holden felt hurt and angry. He asked Stradlater again and again what had happened with Jane, but Stradlater refused to answer and only smiled.",
"Holden could not stop asking, and finally he attacked Stradlater without thinking. Stradlater was much stronger, and he easily won the fight. Holden ended up with a bloody nose and a swollen lip, and Ackley came to look, seeming almost pleased about it.",
"That night, alone and upset, Holden decided suddenly that he would leave Pencey Prep early, instead of waiting for the end of term. He packed his bags quickly. Before he left the dormitory, he shouted a long, loud goodbye down the corridor, so that everyone could hear him."
],
3:[
"On the train to New York, Holden sat next to the mother of a boy from Pencey, a classmate nobody really liked. Holden did not tell her the truth. Instead, he told kind lies about her son, saying he was popular and modest, just to make her happy.",
"In New York, Holden checked into the Edmont Hotel, a shabby, strange place. From his window, he watched other guests behave in odd ways, and it made him feel lonely rather than curious.",
"He telephoned a woman that a friend had once recommended, hoping to talk to someone, but the call did not go well. Later, he went down to the hotel bar and danced with three tourists from Seattle.",
"The three women were not very interesting to talk to. They only wanted to discuss film stars they might see in New York. Still, Holden danced with them and paid for their drinks, mostly because he did not want to be alone.",
"After the women left, Holden sat by himself and thought about Jane Gallagher again. He remembered how they used to play checkers together, and how Jane never moved her kings from the back row, just to keep them safe.",
"He also remembered that Jane never wanted to talk about her stepfather. Something about him clearly upset her, though she never explained exactly what it was, and Holden had never asked her directly."
],
4:[
"In a taxi to Ernie's jazz club, Holden asked the driver, a man named Horwitz, where the ducks in Central Park went during the winter. Horwitz did not know the answer, and the two of them argued about it for the whole ride.",
"At Ernie's, the club was crowded and loud, full of jazz music and people showing off. Holden saw a talkative girl he knew from before, and he quickly left to escape a long, boring conversation with her and her date.",
"Back at the Edmont Hotel, the elevator man, Maurice, offered Holden a prostitute for five dollars. Holden felt nervous but agreed, mostly because he did not want to seem afraid or inexperienced.",
"A young woman named Sunny arrived at his room. Holden suddenly felt too nervous and sad to do anything. He only wanted to talk with her for a while, so he paid her and she left.",
"Later that night, Maurice and Sunny came back together, saying that Holden owed more money. Holden refused to pay more, and Maurice punched him hard in the stomach, leaving him on the floor, hurt and frightened.",
"Alone afterward, Holden imagined that he had been shot, like in a film, and pictured himself acting bravely in front of everyone. Lying there, feeling lonely, he also thought quietly about his dead brother, Allie."
],
5:[
"One morning, Holden had breakfast in a small restaurant and met two nuns. He talked with them about books and enjoyed their company. Before they left, he donated some money to their charity collection, even though he did not have very much himself.",
"Later, Holden bought a record for his little sister, Phoebe. He walked through the park, hoping to see her skating there, but she was not around.",
"He visited the museum he had loved as a child. He remembered how comforting it felt that nothing inside the museum ever changes, even though everything outside it always does. He remembered visiting it many times as a boy, always finding the same quiet rooms exactly as he had left them. Still, he did not go in that day.",
"That afternoon, Holden picked up Sally Hayes, a pretty, conventional girl, for their date. They watched a play together, and afterward they went ice skating at Radio City, even though neither of them skated very well.",
"Sitting and talking after skating, Holden suddenly suggested that they should leave everything behind and live simply in the countryside, in Vermont or Massachusetts. Sally thought the idea was completely unrealistic and told him so clearly.",
"Holden became angry and insulted her, saying cruel things he did not really mean. Sally became upset and almost cried, and the date ended badly, with both of them feeling hurt."
],
6:[
"That evening, Holden watched a film at Radio City that he despised, thinking it was silly and sentimental, full of tears that felt fake to him. Afterward, he went for drinks with Carl Luce, an older schoolmate he used to know at another school.",
"Carl Luce did not enjoy Holden's endless questions about love and adult life, and he left early. Before going, he suggested that Holden should see a therapist to talk about his problems and understand himself better.",
"Alone and increasingly drunk, Holden stayed at the bar drinking more. Around midnight, he called Sally Hayes from a payphone and talked strangely, upsetting her, even though he had insulted her only days before.",
"After the call, Holden walked unsteadily to Central Park to look for the ducks he had asked the taxi driver about earlier. It was cold and dark, and he could not find any ducks at all.",
"While looking for the pond, Holden accidentally dropped Phoebe's record, and it broke into small pieces on the ground. He felt terrible about it, and he carefully kept the broken pieces anyway, wrapped inside his coat pocket.",
"Sitting alone on a park bench in the rain, Holden imagined his own funeral, with his family standing sadly around him. He also pictured Allie's grave, cold and wet, and felt overwhelmed with sadness."
],
7:[
"Late that night, Holden sneaked quietly into his family's apartment, careful not to wake his parents. He went straight to Phoebe's room and gently woke her up.",
"Phoebe, ten years old, clever and affectionate, was happy to see her brother at first. Then she quickly realized, from small details, that Holden had been expelled from Pencey again.",
"Phoebe challenged Holden, telling him that he never liked anything or anyone completely. Thinking hard, Holden tried to explain what he really wanted to be, even though it sounded strange.",
"He told Phoebe he had once misunderstood a song about someone coming through a field of rye. He said he imagined thousands of children playing in a huge field of rye near a cliff, and that his only job would be to catch them gently before they fell over the edge.",
"Before he left, Holden gave Phoebe his red hunting hat as a small, quiet gift. Then he slipped out again to spend the night at the home of Mr. Antolini, one of his favorite former teachers.",
"Mr. Antolini spoke seriously with Holden, warning him kindly that he seemed to be heading toward some kind of terrible fall in his life. Later that night, Holden suddenly woke up and found Mr. Antolini gently stroking his hair. Frightened and confused, Holden panicked, quickly gathered his things, and left the apartment."
],
8:[
"That night, Holden slept for a while in Grand Central Station because he had nowhere else to go. He felt increasingly ill and tired, and he began planning a strange, impossible idea: to hitchhike out west and live alone as a deaf-mute, away from everyone.",
"In the morning, he went to Phoebe's school to leave her a short note about his plan. On the stairs, he saw an obscenity someone had written on the wall, and it enraged him deeply, because he did not want Phoebe or other young children to see something so ugly.",
"Holden went to wait for Phoebe near the museum, as they had planned. When she arrived, she was pulling a heavy suitcase behind her, completely determined to go west with him instead of staying home.",
"Holden refused firmly to let her come with him, and this led to a painful argument between them. Phoebe became extremely upset and would not speak to him at all afterward.",
"To make her feel better, Holden suggested they walk to the zoo and then to the carousel nearby. Slowly, Phoebe's anger softened, and she agreed to ride the carousel while Holden watched from a bench.",
"It began to rain heavily, but Holden did not move. Wearing his red hunting hat, he watched Phoebe go around and around, reaching happily for the gold ring on the carousel, and for a moment, sitting there in the rain, he felt suddenly, completely happy.",
"Telling this whole story from the rest home, Holden admitted that he missed nearly everybody he had talked about: Stradlater, Ackley, even Maurice, in a strange way. He said that talking about people can make you miss them, even people you did not like very much."
]
};
